        {
          "thinking": "With both files in hand, I'm putting together a thorough answer covering how the rules read lamports via the AccountInfo lamports() method, comparing before/after values captured locally with saturating subtraction and safe u128 comparisons to avoid overflow, and checking whether the handler actually asserts a lamport change across the call, since withdraw logic writes through try_borrow_mut_lamports in several places.\n\nMultiple rules in withdrawals_fee_sharing.rs assert exact lamport deltas -- ensuring vault balances decrease correctly, lamports are conserved across the three accounts, fees compute exactly to 100 bps when a collector exists, no fee is taken without one, the fee collector never increases the authority's payout, and fee evasion or desync attempts are caught. Similarly, vault_initialization.rs has rules ensuring balance stays less than or equal to vault lamports both at initialization and after withdrawal. I also note the direct comment confirming that withdraw is the one handler moving lamports without a CPI, making both sides of the inequality observable through the shared Rc<RefCell<..>> account state.\n\nThe module's top comment makes this explicit: a cross-program invocation moves nothing in the prover's model, which is exactly why the deposit invariant is checked against the accounting core rather than the handler itself, and why the vault's lamports appear unchanged across initialize in every rule. This confirms CPI-based transfers (used in deposit/initialize) are invisible to the prover, while withdraw's direct try_borrow_mut_lamports write is the one observable lamport change. I'm ready to write the final answer now.\n\n",
          "type": "thinking",
          "index": 0,
          "signature": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "## How the two spec files read and assert over account lamports\n\nBoth `vault_initialization.rs` and `withdrawals_fee_sharing.rs` get their accounts from `Box::new(cvlr_deserialize_nondet_accounts())` and then call the plain `AccountInfo::lamports()` getter directly on `accounts[i]` (never on the Anchor-wrapped `ix.vault`, `ix.authority`, etc.), capturing a `before` value, invoking the real handler, then capturing an `after` value and comparing them. Deltas are computed defensively \u2014 with `saturating_sub` (never bare `-`) and widened to `u128` before equating, to avoid panicking/pruning wrong-direction executions and to avoid overflow in sums.\n\nRepresentative patterns:\n\n```rust\nlet v_before = accounts[0].lamports();\n...\nlet ctx = Context::new(&crate::ID, &mut ix, &[], Default::default());\ncrate::vault_program::withdraw(ctx, amount).unwrap();\nlet v_after = accounts[0].lamports();\ncvlr_assert!(v_after as u128 + amount as u128 == v_before as u128);\n```\n\n```rust\ncvlr_assert!(v_after <= v_before);\ncvlr_assert!(a_after >= a_before);\ncvlr_assert!(c_after >= c_before);\nlet out = v_before.saturating_sub(v_after);\nlet in_a = a_after.saturating_sub(a_before);\nlet in_c = c_after.saturating_sub(c_before);\ncvlr_assert!(out as u128 == in_a as u128 + in_c as u128);\ncvlr_assert!(out == amount);\n```\n\n## Yes \u2014 several rules assert a lamport value changed across the `withdraw` call\n\nIn **`withdrawals_fee_sharing.rs`**, rules built directly around lamport deltas across `crate::vault_program::withdraw(...)` (which internally does `**vault_info.try_borrow_mut_lamports()? -= amount` etc.):\n\n- `rule_withdraw_vault_lamports_decrease_by_exactly_amount_with_collector` / `_no_collector` \u2014 asserts `v_after + amount == v_before`.\n- `rule_withdraw_conserves_lamports_across_the_three_accounts` \u2014 asserts vault decreases and authority/collector increase, and the sum is conserved and equals `amount`.\n- `rule_withdraw_fee_is_exactly_100_bps_when_collector_supplied` / `rule_withdraw_takes_no_fee_when_collector_absent` \u2014 asserts authority/collector lamport increases equal the fee split.\n- `rule_fee_collector_never_increases_authority_payout` \u2014 compares two separate withdraw invocations' authority lamport deltas.\n- `rule_fee_evasion_by_passing_authority_as_fee_collector` and `rule_vault_as_fee_collector_desyncs_balance_from_lamports` \u2014 exploit account aliasing (passing the same `AccountInfo` as both authority/fee_collector or vault/fee_collector) and assert on the resulting shared lamport-cell delta.\n\nIn **`vault_initialization.rs`**, the induction step for `withdraw` (`rule_withdraw_preserves_balance_le_vault_lamports`) reads `accounts[0].lamports()`/`accounts[1].lamports()`/`accounts[2].lamports()` before and after calling `withdraw`, and asserts `balance_after <= lamports_after` \u2014 an invariant that depends on the vault's lamports having actually decreased by the handler's direct write.\n\n## Explicit comment/evidence on observability of `try_borrow_mut_lamports` writes vs. CPI transfers\n\n`vault_initialization.rs` states this directly as design rationale, distinguishing the two lamport-movement mechanisms in the program:\n\n```\n/// The inductive step for `withdraw`: assuming the invariant holds on entry, it still holds on\n/// exit. `withdraw` is the one handler that moves the vault's lamports *without* a CPI \u2014\n/// `**vault_info.try_borrow_mut_lamports()? -= amount` \u2014 so both sides of the inequality are\n/// observable here through the account's shared `Rc<RefCell<..>>`.\n```\n\nAnd, contrastingly, for CPI-based transfers (used by `deposit`/`initialize`), the module's top-level doc explicitly says the Prover treats a CPI as a no-op, so lamport changes performed by the System Program are *not* observable:\n\n```\n/// * A cross-program invocation moves nothing in the Prover's model. That is why the `deposit`\n///   step of the balance invariant is stated against the accounting core rather than the handler,\n///   and why the vault's lamports are unchanged across `initialize` in every rule below.\n```\n\nand, for the `deposit` inductive-step rule specifically:\n\n```\n/// Why the descent: `deposit` credits the vault through a System Program `transfer` CPI, and the\n/// Prover models a CPI as changing nothing. A handler-level rule would therefore see the recorded\n/// balance rise while the observed lamports stayed put, and would report a violation that no real\n/// execution can produce. Here the lamports the handler asked the System Program to move are added\n/// arithmetically instead \u2014 the handler passes one and the same `amount` to\n/// `system_instruction::transfer` and to `apply_deposit`.\n```\n\nSo: **direct in-handler lamport mutation via `try_borrow_mut_lamports` (as done in `withdraw`) is observable to the prover** \u2014 many rules in both files read `accounts[i].lamports()` before/after `withdraw` and assert exact deltas/conservation/fee-splitting. **Lamport movement performed only via a CPI (System Program `transfer`, used by `deposit`/`initialize`'s rent funding) is *not* observable** \u2014 the prover models CPIs as no-ops, which is why the deposit/initialize lamport-invariant rules are stated arithmetically against the accounting core (`apply_deposit`) rather than against observed `lamports()` deltas, and why the module doc for `vault_initialization.rs` explicitly notes \"the vault's lamports are unchanged across `initialize` in every rule below.\"",
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1
        }
