        {
          "text": "Based on full inspection of both files, here is the answer.",
          "type": "text",
          "index": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "toolu_01Km8QKxuGPLndSPKrFjw3Kc",
          "input": {},
          "name": "result",
          "type": "tool_use",
          "caller": {
            "type": "direct"
          },
          "index": 1,
          "partial_json": "{\"value\": \"Both `deposits_balance_accounting.rs` and `withdrawals_fee_sharing.rs` import the same helper:\\n\\n```rust\\nuse cvlr_solana::cvlr_deserialize_nondet_accounts;\\n```\\n\\n**Where it's defined / return type.** `cvlr_deserialize_nondet_accounts` is *not* defined anywhere in this repository \u2014 it comes from the external `cvlr-solana` crate (pinned in the workspace root `Cargo.toml` as `cvlr-solana = \\\"=0.5.0\\\"`, pulled in only under the vault crate's `certora` feature: `dep:cvlr-solana`). No vendored copy of `cvlr-solana`'s source exists in the VFS, so its exact declared signature can't be quoted from this codebase; it must be treated as an external, unverified fact. What can be established from usage in both files is:\\n\\n- It's called with no arguments and no turbofish/type parameter (`cvlr_deserialize_nondet_accounts()`), and its result is immediately heap-allocated: `let accounts = Box::new(cvlr_deserialize_nondet_accounts());`. This \\\"box it\\\" pattern is used identically ~5 times in `deposits_balance_accounting.rs` (including inside the `deposit_accounts!` macro) and ~13 times in `withdrawals_fee_sharing.rs`.\\n- The boxed value is indexable (`accounts[0]`, `accounts[1]`, \u2026), each element behaving as an `AccountInfo` (methods `.data_len()`, `.lamports()`, `.key`, `.is_signer` are called on `accounts[i]`, and elements are fed into `Account::try_from(&accounts[i])`, `Signer::try_from(&accounts[i])`, `Program::try_from(&accounts[i])`, `UncheckedAccount::try_from(&accounts[i])`), and it also coerces to a slice: in `withdrawals_fee_sharing.rs`, `let mut rest: &[AccountInfo] = &accounts[..];` is passed straight into `Withdraw::try_accounts(&crate::ID, &mut rest, &[], &mut bumps, &mut reallocs)`. So the return type is effectively (a `Box`-wrapped) fixed-size collection of `AccountInfo<'_>` values \u2014 most consistent with a fixed-length array `[AccountInfo; N]` for some crate-internal `N`, since it's boxed rather than being a `Vec` explicitly constructed here, but the concrete `N`/type is only visible in the external crate.\\n\\n**How many accounts it yields.** The count isn't stated anywhere in the vault crate's spec files; it's a property of the external helper. However, usage bounds a lower limit: in `deposits_balance_accounting.rs` only indices `0..=2` are ever used (`Deposit`/`Initialize`/`Withdraw` accounts structs each need at most 3 accounts: vault, depositor/authority, system_program/fee_collector). In `withdrawals_fee_sharing.rs`, `rule_fee_collector_never_increases_authority_payout` builds *two* separate `Withdraw` account sets out of one call's accounts and indexes up to `accounts[4]` (`accounts[3]` = second vault, `accounts[4]` = second authority), so the helper must yield at least 5 nondeterministic accounts per call. Neither file declares or overrides that count itself \u2014 it's whatever `cvlr_deserialize_nondet_accounts` produces by default in `cvlr-solana` 0.5.0.\\n\\n**Stand-in / mock functions.**\\n\\n- `deposits_balance_accounting.rs` **does** define stand-ins, matching the pattern already established for that module: a `pub fn invoke(instruction: &Instruction, _account_infos: &[AccountInfo]) -> ProgramResult` that records call/success counters and a key-fingerprint of `instruction.program_id` into `static mut` globals (`INVOKE_CALLS`, `INVOKE_OK_CALLS`, `INVOKE_PROGRAM_0..3`, `INVOKE_SHOULD_FAIL`) instead of performing a real CPI, and a `pub mod system_instruction { pub fn transfer(from: &Pubkey, to: &Pubkey, lamports: u64) -> Instruction { ... } }` that records `(from, to, lamports)` into similarly-named `static mut` word-pairs (`TRANSFER_CALLS`, `TRANSFER_FROM_0..3`, `TRANSFER_TO_0..3`, `TRANSFER_LAMPORTS`) and returns a hand-built `Instruction { program_id: anchor_lang::solana_program::system_program::ID, accounts: Vec::new(), data: Vec::new() }`. (This version differs cosmetically from an earlier described one only in storing pubkeys as four `u64` words per key instead of `[u8; 32]`/single `u64` fields \u2014 the module's own comment explains this was changed because `[u8;32]` globals came back unconstrained in the prover.) These stand-ins are what `crate::vault_program::deposit`'s `use`s are pointed at under the `unit_deposits_balance_accounting` cargo feature (per `lib.rs`), since `deposit` performs a real System-Program CPI that the prover cannot model.\\n\\n- `withdrawals_fee_sharing.rs` **does not** define any `invoke`/`system_instruction` (or other CPI) stand-in. Its module doc explicitly notes why none is needed: `withdraw`'s handler \\\"needs none of the CPI machinery: it moves lamports with direct `try_borrow_mut_lamports` writes,\\\" so there is no CPI to intercept. This file's only helper functions are pure spec utilities local to it: `spec_fee_u128`/`spec_fee` (the independently-restated 100-bps fee formula), `pk_lo` (first 8 bytes of a `Pubkey`, for logging), and `outcome_is_ok` (reduces a `Result<()>` to `bool` via `core::mem::forget` to avoid dropping an unanalyzable `Error`) \u2014 none of which mock a CPI or any external program.\\n\\nSummary: only `deposits_balance_accounting.rs` needs and provides `invoke`/`system_instruction::transfer` mocks (because `deposit` CPIs to the System Program); `withdrawals_fee_sharing.rs` defines no such stand-ins because `withdraw` never performs a CPI. Both files use the identical `Box::new(cvlr_deserialize_nondet_accounts())` pattern to obtain nondeterministic account infos, indexed like an array/slice of `AccountInfo`; the exact declared return type and total account count are properties of the external `cvlr-solana` crate not present in this repository, but usage in `withdrawals_fee_sharing.rs` shows at least 5 accounts are available per invocation.\"}"
        }
